::Q1::What problem does AWS IAM solve? 
{
=It controls who can access AWS resources and what actions they are allowed to perform
~It stores application files
~It runs virtual machines
~It provides relational database tables
}

::Q2::What is an IAM group used for? 
{
=Organizing users so permissions can be managed for the group
~Storing S3 objects
~Running EC2 workloads
~Creating database replicas
}

::Q3::What is an IAM policy? 
{
=A document defining allowed or denied actions on specified resources
~A database backup
~An EC2 machine image
~An S3 object itself
}

::Q4::Why are overly broad IAM permissions risky? 
{
=They can allow mistakes or compromised credentials to cause greater damage
~They make S3 objects smaller
~They reduce database storage capacity
~They prevent EC2 from starting
}

::Q5::How can a narrowly scoped IAM policy be verified? 
{
=Confirm permitted actions succeed and actions outside the policy fail
~Make every action succeed
~Remove the policy before testing
~Grant administrator access during verification
}